# Consequences, not activity

The operator said it plainly: *"never ever never going to read all that"*. Every reply answers,
in order: what changed, what it means for the hardware in the woods, what the operator has to
do — say "nothing" when that's true. Do not reproduce what is already recorded: commits,
ADRs, and the changelog hold the reasoning; cite a file path instead of quoting its content.

# Never make the operator decode a label

Say the thing, not its filing code. `h1`, `C5`, `D11`, `P0.14`, `ADR-0013`, `#102`, `H1–H8`,
`0c` — these are indexes into documents the operator is not holding, and a sentence built from
them is unreadable at the moment it is read. This is the single most common way a reply becomes
"never going to read all that".

| Don't | Do |
|---|---|
| "h1 is measured, h2 unblocked" | "The pin 3 / pin 5 capture is done. The clamp design can start." |
| "C5 changed default_envs" | "`pio run` was building for the wrong pin; it isn't now." |
| "ADR-0013 is superseded" | "The decision to reserve pin 3 no longer holds — both pins carry the fault." |
| "H1–H8 remain open" | "None of the eight hardening gates has been met." |

The identifier may follow the plain words when it helps someone find the record — "the decision
reserving pin 3 (`ADR-0013`)" — but it never stands alone, and never leads. Same for a bare
commit SHA or issue number: name what it is first.

Inside `docs/`, commit messages, and code comments the identifiers are the record and stay as
they are. This rule governs what is said **to the operator**.

# Never explain the operator's own hardware back to them

They built these nodes and have stated the wiring repeatedly; it is settled in
`docs/HARDWARE.md` — cite the file, do not recite the table. Before any sentence about the
hardware, ask: does the operator already know this? If yes, delete it — not shorten, delete.
Same for the trivially obvious: an image built with the A1 flag uses the A1 pin; saying so as a
finding insults the reader. One exception: a **new** hazard discovered this session — say it
once, then write it into `docs/HARDWARE.md` so it never needs saying again.
